## Exploring the Night Sky and Earth from Space

The Radio Astronomy and Earth Observation Division brings together astronomy lovers, physics majors, and curious students who want to look at the cosmos and explore satellite images of our home planet.

From setting up optical telescopes on campus grounds during clear nights to learning how to catch cosmic radio waves with DIY antennas, we make astronomy hands-on and social.

## What We Do in Our Club

### 1. Stargazing Nights and Telescope Sessions
There is nothing quite like looking through an eyepiece and seeing the rings of Saturn for the first time:
- Organizing campus stargazing nights for students to view the Moon, planets, and bright nebulae
- Learning how to align, focus, and take care of reflective and refractive telescopes
- Trying out smartphone astrophotography through telescope lenses to capture crisp lunar craters

### 2. DIY Radio Astronomy and Signal Catching
Did you know you can listen to the sky even on cloudy days? We experiment with simple radio setups:
- Building basic wire antennas to pick up solar radio bursts and natural radio noise from Jupiter
- Exploring Software Defined Radio (SDR) software to visualize radio spectrum signals on laptops
- Understanding how radio astronomy works and why it allows scientists to map our galaxy

### 3. Open Satellite Imagery and Mapping
Earth observation satellites take incredible images every single day, and the data is free for anyone to use:
- Accessing open satellite imagery from Sentinel and Landsat missions
- Creating simple maps of Sri Lanka showing vegetation health, forest cover, and water reservoirs
- Learning beginner friendly tools like QGIS and Google Earth Engine to spot environmental changes

## What You Get as a Student Member

- Memorable Stargazing Nights: Join outdoor observation sessions and night sky photography workshops.
- Fun Data Projects: Learn to work with real astronomical data and satellite maps that boost your resume.
- Great Conversations: Hang out with passionate fellow students who love talking about black holes, exoplanets, and space exploration.
